WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.872
A secure Minimal API baseline starts with
organization.

2
00:00:05.952 --> 00:00:12.779
Keep endpoints grouped by purpose and
sensitivity so policy is visible.

3
00:00:12.859 --> 00:00:19.239
Use route groups, names, tags, and
metadata to make public, tenant,

4
00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:26.258
internal, administrative, and operational
surfaces easier to inspect.

5
00:00:26.338 --> 00:00:33.724
Authenticate explicitly and authorize with
clear group and endpoint policy.

6
00:00:33.804 --> 00:00:37.609
Authentication identifies the caller.

7
00:00:37.689 --> 00:00:42.054
Authorization decides what the caller may
do.

8
00:00:42.134 --> 00:00:46.051
Group-level rules improve consistency,

9
00:00:46.131 --> 00:00:51.392
but sensitive endpoints still need
individual review,

10
00:00:51.472 --> 00:00:57.291
especially when records, tenants, exports,
configuration,

11
00:00:57.371 --> 00:01:01.512
or administrative workflows are involved.

12
00:01:01.592 --> 00:01:04.614
Treat bound input as untrusted.

13
00:01:04.694 --> 00:01:12.305
Parameter binding creates handler
parameters from route, query, header, body,

14
00:01:12.385 --> 00:01:19.100
form, service, or custom sources, but it
does not prove the data is safe.

15
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:26.007
Use DTOs to define input boundaries, avoid
broad domain-model binding,

16
00:01:26.087 --> 00:01:30.452
validate required fields and business
rules,

17
00:01:30.532 --> 00:01:35.008
and use filters carefully for reusable
checks.

18
00:01:35.088 --> 00:01:39.118
Shape responses and errors deliberately.

19
00:01:39.198 --> 00:01:44.234
Use response DTOs to avoid exposing
internal fields,

20
00:01:44.314 --> 00:01:51.029
keep error responses useful but limited,
use consistent status codes,

21
00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:58.719
and preserve detailed diagnostics in
protected logs rather than client-facing

22
00:01:58.799 --> 00:01:59.919
responses.

23
00:01:59.999 --> 00:02:06.378
Finally, review cross-cutting controls and
production exposure.

24
00:02:06.458 --> 00:02:13.733
CORS, CSRF prevention, rate limits,
request body limits, security headers,

25
00:02:13.813 --> 00:02:18.290
OpenAPI documentation, logging,
monitoring,

26
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:23.854
and deployment settings all affect Minimal
API security.

27
00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:31.208
Minimal code can still support a strong
baseline when the team keeps security

28
00:02:31.288 --> 00:02:37.220
decisions explicit, consistent, and
operationally visible.
